  content: `
<h2>Why English deserves real study time</h2>
<p>Everyone writes English, and most candidates assume they will do fine because they speak it daily. That is exactly why marks leak. Everyday English and exam English are different. Questions ask about precise meaning, fixed expressions and grammar rules that we break in normal talk.</p>
<p>The good news is that English is among the most trainable subjects. The same patterns repeat from year to year. If you practise each section deliberately, a high score is realistic. Check the current JAMB syllabus for the exact breakdown, then spend your time where the marks are.</p>

<h2>Comprehension: read smart, not slow</h2>
<p>Comprehension passages feel long under time pressure. The solution is a method, not speed reading.</p>
[[flow Read the questions first ; Skim the passage for the main idea ; Scan for keywords from each question ; Re-read that sentence carefully ; Choose and move on]]
<h3>Rules that save marks</h3>
<ul>
<li>The answer is always supported by the passage. If you need outside knowledge to choose, you are probably wrong.</li>
<li>Watch for extreme words such as always, never, only and all. Options with them are often traps.</li>
<li>For questions on the writer's attitude or purpose, look at the tone words, such as praise, complaint or warning.</li>
<li>For vocabulary-in-context questions, replace the word with each option in the sentence and see which one fits the meaning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Lexis and structure: the biggest scoring area</h2>
<p>This section tests vocabulary, idioms and grammar in short sentences. The questions are quick, so it is where you can collect marks fast.</p>
<h3>Vocabulary</h3>
<p>Synonyms and antonyms are tested in context. Do not learn a long list blindly. Learn words in sentences, and group them by theme. For example, group words for being unhappy, such as sad, gloomy and downcast.</p>
<h3>Idioms</h3>
<p>Idioms cannot be worked out from the words. You must know them. Read a few each day with their meanings, and use each in your own sentence.</p>
<h3>Grammar</h3>
<p>The most common patterns are subject-verb agreement, tenses, prepositions, and word forms. Here is a typical item.</p>
[[qa Choose the correct option: Each of the students ____ a textbook.|Find the subject: Each, which is singular even though students is nearby ; A singular subject needs a singular verb ; Options such as have are plural, so reject them|has]]
<p>Notice the trap: the plural word students sits right before the blank. Examiners love placing a plural noun near a singular subject. Always find the real subject first.</p>

<h2>Oral English: sounds and stress</h2>
<p>Oral English questions are often the ones candidates skip. They test whether you can tell sounds apart, such as the vowel in sheep and ship, and which syllable carries the stress in a word.</p>
<p>You cannot learn this only from reading. Say words aloud, look up pronunciation in a dictionary and listen to clear speakers. A few minutes of this each day builds an ear that no cramming can replace. Learn the common stress patterns, like how many two-syllable nouns stress the first syllable while verbs stress the second, such as the noun record and the verb record, and then test yourself with questions.</p>

<h2>The prescribed text, if your year includes one</h2>
<p>Some years include a literature text as part of the English paper. Check the current JAMB syllabus and brochure to see whether one applies and which book it is. If it does, read the book itself, not only summaries. Know the plot, the main characters, the themes and a few key events. Questions often ask who said something, what it reveals or what lesson the story teaches.</p>

<h2>A simple daily routine for English</h2>
<p>Thirty to forty minutes a day is enough if it is varied.</p>
[[timeline Day 1::Two comprehension passages with a timer ; Day 2::20 lexis and structure questions plus five new idioms ; Day 3::Oral English: sounds and stress practice ; Day 4::Grammar rules and error log review ; Day 5::Mixed set under time ; Weekend::Redo every question you missed this week]]
[[callout tip|Keep an English error log|Every time you miss a grammar or vocabulary item, write the correct form and a short rule. Re-reading this list is a faster way to improve than any new textbook.]]

<h2>Common leaks and how to plug them</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choosing what sounds right:</strong> Familiar mistakes sound natural in Nigerian speech. Check the rule instead of trusting your ear.</li>
<li><strong>Rushing the last questions:</strong> Do not leave the oral section to the end with two minutes left. Decide a time budget per section before you start.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring the small words:</strong> Prepositions such as in, on and at often decide the answer. Learn set phrases like different from and interested in.</li>
<li><strong>Not reading all the options:</strong> The first option that looks right is sometimes only partly right.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to do today</h2>
<p>Start with the part where you are weakest. Your last practice set will tell you which one it is.</p>
[[checklist Do one comprehension passage using the read-questions-first method ; Answer 15 lexis and structure questions and log every miss ; Learn five idioms with meanings and write a sentence for each ; Say ten words aloud and check their stress in a dictionary ; Confirm from the current JAMB syllabus whether a prescribed text applies]]
`
